  <h2 id="resumen">Executive summary</h2>
  <p>
    Nakire TPS connects the physical operation of a warehouse with the software that
    coordinates it. The system brings people, applications and industrial devices
    —robots, PLCs, LEDs, picking, cameras and sound— into one workflow, with
    traceability and the ability to evolve warehouse by warehouse.
  </p>
  <p>
    The solution was organised around domains and services with clear
    responsibilities, event-driven communication and real-time channels to reflect
    the state of operations. My work combined architecture, domain modelling,
    service development, device integration and the definition of communication
    flows.
  </p>
  <p>
    It was built in two phases: an MVP between April and July 2025, and a second
    phase from September 2025 to June 2026.
  </p>

  <h2 id="origen">Where the architecture comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Nakire TPS is an in-house product. What comes from earlier is the way of thinking
    about the system, not its code: the first engine I wrote was Basetis's search
    engine, with bracketed queries —AND, OR, NOT, NOR— and a regular-expression
    validator. At LoveISDIN I led the frontend and met DDD and hexagonal
    architecture on the backend; the visual side, which until then had grown into
    huge components, moved to Atomic Design with an extra layer: particles, atoms,
    molecules, organisms, layouts and pages.
  </p>
  <p>
    In <a href="/en/projects/bodega-digital/">Bodega Digital</a> I brought that
    backend architecture into the frontend for the first time, inside a monolith:
    one deployment and domain boundaries in the code. Nakire TPS takes the idea to
    microservices: each device family with its own service, and a domain core that
    knows the tasks and rules shared by every module.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contexto">Industrial context</h2>
  <p>
    In an automated warehouse, moving a pallet is not an isolated operation: it
    involves a task, an available device, an operator who needs to know what to do
    and a record of what happened. The software has to coordinate those actors
    without becoming a bottleneck and without losing sight of who can do what.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every warehouse arrives with its own map, robot versions and business rules.
    The system had to absorb that variation without multiplying code or depending on
    a specific vendor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="problema">The problem</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Coordinate tasks across very different device families, each with its own
      protocol and life cycle.
    </li>
    <li>
      Let a warehouse's configuration —map, robots, versions— change without
      rewriting the domain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reflect the operation state in real time so people can act on what is
      happening.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep clear boundaries between what the business decides and what the hardware
      decides.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="restricciones">Constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Heterogeneous hardware.</strong> Forklifts, PLCs, LEDs, picking and
      cameras share no model, version or protocol.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Validation on real hardware.</strong> The pipeline's QA gates ran on
      physical devices, not only on mocks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloud deployment over VPN.</strong> The software lives in the cloud
      and connects to the industrial installation through a VPN.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Vendors and documentation.</strong> Technical communication and
      documentation with Chinese vendors were in English.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Traceability and roles.</strong> Each operator should see and execute
      only what their role allowed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rol">My role</h2>
  <p>
    My work combined architecture design, domain modelling, service development,
    device integration, definition of communication flows and support for technical
    decisions. I was part of a team: what follows distinguishes what I designed and
    led from what was collective work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      I designed and implemented an event-driven system on DDD, TDD and
      microservices, with a monolith grouped into frontend, backend, core and a
      subset of devices.
    </li>
    <li>
      I defined the agent core that orders, prioritises and distributes work across
      available devices.
    </li>
    <li>
      I separated each device family into its own microservice, with adapters per
      model, version and protocol.
    </li>
    <li>
      I defined the reactive WebSocket frontend and access control by role and
      operator type.
    </li>
    <li>
      I supported deployment in Docker on cloud over VPN, with CI/CD and QA gates on
      real physical hardware.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="arquitectura">Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    The frontend and the API talk to a core that knows the tasks; the core does not
    know each device's protocols, only its capabilities.
  </p>

  <ArchitectureDiagram />

  <p class="diagram-alt">
    In text: the frontend connects to the API; the API delegates to the agent core
    —dictator, ranker and distributor—; the core publishes and consumes events on
    the bus; each device family has its own microservice with adapters per model,
    version and protocol; and those services talk to the warehouse's real hardware.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dominios">Domains and services</h2>
  <p>
    DDD and TDD were used to absorb the business rules, maps and robot versions
    that differ per warehouse. Abstraction happened through use cases, ports and
    adapters: changing an API or a device does not drag the domain along.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core works with tasks at different levels of detail, from a task pointing to
    a concrete device to one that only expresses the family and the action. That
    granularity lets the system keep coordinating when a robot model is unavailable
    or when a warehouse adds a new version.
  </p>
  <p>
    Agents distribute the work: <strong>dictator</strong> orders,
    <strong>ranker</strong> prioritises and <strong>distributor</strong> delegates.
    The goal is to maximise device availability and minimise the movements needed
    to complete tasks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each module had its own API, backoffice and frontend, and shared a DDD core with
    the rest: business rules lived in the module's domain. Role-based access control
    crossed the entire system, so the same operator saw what corresponded to them in
    any module.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eventos">Events and communication</h2>
  <p>
    Communication between services is asynchronous and event-driven. The system's
    event bus lets the core publish what it has decided and each service react
    without the producer knowing its consumers. Adding a device family or a new
    capability does not require touching the core.
  </p>
  <p>
    That separation also contains failure: a device service that stops responding
    does not block coordination for the rest, although the system must decide what
    to do with the tasks that depended on it. Persistence follows the same division:
    time series for metrics, PostgreSQL and Redis for transactions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reglas">System rules</h2>
  <p>
    A use case may emit the events the business decides, but each event is consumed
    by a single use case. The rule looks small and changes the whole system: it
    leaves a clean trace of who did what and a single failure point, always in the
    same place, through which every event passes.
  </p>
  <p>
    That traceability was designed for operations: when a task does not move
    forward, there is one place to look. With the same logic, real time is declared
    in the use case.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hardware">Hardware integration</h2>
  <p>
    Each device family —forklift, PLC, LED, picking, camera— is its own
    microservice, with adapters per model, version and protocol. The domain
    expresses the intention; the adapter translates it into the concrete protocol.
  </p>
  <p>
    That boundary was the decision that paid for itself most often: a new vendor or
    a different robot version joined as an adapter, not as a change to business
    rules. It also made it possible to document and communicate integrations in
    English with vendors without mixing their vocabulary with the domain's.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tiempo-real">Real time and roles</h2>
  <p>
    The frontend is reactive and communicates over WebSocket: the interface reflects
    the operation state without the operator having to refresh. RBAC applies over
    that channel, so the interface adapts to the role —admin or operator— and to the
    operator type.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence is that the operator sees the next action that
    corresponds to them, with the context of what is happening in the warehouse,
    instead of a generic screen with every possibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    Besides WebSocket, the system used SSE for delta updates: the use case decides
    whether a state change —a task moving from PENDING to IN_PROGRESS— deserves a
    send, publishes it on the bus and the handler distributes it to listeners
    subscribed to that task. The client declares the subscription when opening the
    task.
  </p>

  <h2 id="despliegue">CI/CD and deployment</h2>
  <p>
    The platform is deployed with Docker and Kubernetes in the cloud, under a VPN to
    the installation. The pipeline includes CI/CD and deterministic QA gates on real
    physical hardware: validation did not stop at the integration environment, it
    reached the devices that later operate in the warehouse.
  </p>

  <h2 id="decisiones">Technical decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Agent core instead of rigid orchestration.</strong> It allows work to
      be prioritised and distributed by availability, in exchange for assuming state
      and idempotency in tasks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>One microservice per device family.</strong> It isolates protocols and
      versions, in exchange for more services to deploy and observe.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tasks with variable granularity.</strong> The same model serves a
      concrete device and an entire family, in exchange for a richer task contract.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>DDD and TDD for the domain.</strong> They absorb rules that change per
      warehouse, in exchange for initial modelling effort that is recovered with
      every variant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>QA on real hardware.</strong> It detects what no mock detects, in
      exchange for a slower pipeline that depends on devices.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="resultados">Outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    The system connected software and industrial operations, improved process
    traceability and automated interactions that previously required manual
    coordination. The outcomes I can state are observable, not invented metrics:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Tasks are recorded and associated with their device and their operator.</li>
    <li>
      The interface reflects the operation state in real time and adapts to the role
      of whoever uses it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Adding a device model or version does not require rebuilding the domain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Deployment and validation are automated, including checks on real hardware.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="aprendizajes">Lessons learned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      In industrial systems, hardware sets the pace: the architecture has to assume
      that devices fail, change version and are not always available.
    </li>
    <li>
      Explicit contracts between domain and device save more time than any
      premature optimisation.
    </li>
    <li>
      Documentation and the vocabulary shared with vendors are part of engineering,
      not paperwork.
    </li>
  </ul>
